# <project>/.claude/hpc.env — machine/domain profile for hpc-submit.
# Sourced (sh) by the submit templates and the bare-server launcher; keep it
# to plain VAR=value lines. This file is the ONLY place machine- and
# domain-library-specific paths live; the plugin scripts stay generic.

# --- developed libraries (user forks a job imports) -------------------------
# ONE declaration per lib serves both halves of DESIGN.md §8.2, and buying
# them separately is not possible: declaring a lib puts its SHA in every log
# header (the record) AND holds both sides to one commit at `remote.sh push`
# (the gate). The half-open state — a SHA in every header, nothing holding the
# sides to it — once recorded eight runs against a commit no remote holds.
#
# One line per side, in THIS committed file — each machine reads its own
# column, so local and remote paths live together and nothing needs a
# per-machine copy:
#
#   LIB_<name>_LOCAL=<path on this, the record-keeping machine>
#   LIB_<name>_<HOSTKEY>=<path on that remote host>
#
# <name> is variable-safe (letters, digits, _; for a pinned fork use the
# tool.uv.sources name so bump_lib.sh finds the checkout). <HOSTKEY> is the
# REMOTE_HOST name with every non-alphanumeric dropped (my-cluster -> mycluster)
# and never contains an underscore. Declare EVERY fork a job imports, one pair
# of lines each — multiple libs are simply multiple declarations:
#
#LIB_mylib_LOCAL=/Users/me/src/mylib
#LIB_mylib_hpc=/home/me/src/mylib
#LIB_otherlib_LOCAL=/Users/me/src/otherlib
#LIB_otherlib_hpc=/home/me/src/otherlib
#
# When tool.uv.sources pins a fork by git commit instead, what runs is
# uv.lock's rev (fetched by uv itself): the push gate steps aside for the pin
# gate, and the LOCAL line doubles as bump_lib.sh's checkout location.
#
# Legacy spellings (DOMAIN_LIBS / DOMAIN_LIB_DIR for the header,
# LIB_CHECKOUTS for pins, SYNC_LIBS for the gate) are honoured for one more
# version when no LIB_* line is declared; doctor says how to migrate.

# --- remote HPC (optional; bootstrap --remote host:dir fills these) ---------
# The cluster runs jobs only — no Claude there. Code reaches it via GitHub
# (local push -> remote ff-pull); logs and small data come back via the
# fixer's rsync; the queue snapshot feeds the board and the J3/J4 lint.
# REMOTE_HOST is the DEFAULT host — the one used when an operation names no
# host (and, with REMOTE_HOSTS unset, the only host). REMOTE_DIR is its clone.
#REMOTE_HOST=hpc
#REMOTE_DIR=/path/to/project/clone/on/cluster
#REMOTE_DATA_MAX_MB=50
# Queue placement for this cluster — asked for at setup and applied by the
# submit step, so no submit file carries it. Leaving them unset means every job
# lands in whatever the cluster defaults to, which on a shared partition can be
# an hour of PENDING for a ten-minute job.
#REMOTE_PARTITION=<partition>
#REMOTE_RESERVATION=<group standing reservation, if you have one>
#REMOTE_ACCOUNT=<charge account, if the cluster requires one>

# Multi-remote (optional): declare EVERY cluster in REMOTE_HOSTS — no
# primary/secondary, all listed hosts are monitored equally (remote.sh queue
# and pull loop over the list; queue-snapshot rows are tagged `@<host>`).
# Each host's clone path lives in REMOTE_DIR_<host> (non-alphanumeric chars in
# the ssh alias map to `_` in the variable name); the default host may instead
# fall back to plain REMOTE_DIR. Leave REMOTE_HOSTS unset for the single-host
# contract above — it behaves exactly as before.
#REMOTE_HOSTS="hpc other"
#REMOTE_DIR_hpc=/path/to/project/clone/on/hpc
#REMOTE_DIR_other=/path/to/project/clone/on/other

# The push gate for developed libraries reads the LIB_* declarations above —
# nothing further to declare here. (Legacy SYNC_LIBS is honoured for one more
# version when no LIB_* line exists.)

# Machine-local overrides (e.g. a third ad-hoc machine's own LIB_* paths) go
# in .claude/hpc.local.env — gitignored, so each side keeps its own values.

# --- Julia (optional; unset = the project has no Julia environment) ---------
# The env directory this machine runs, repo-relative. `remote.sh push`
# instantiates it on the target host BEFORE checking it, so a far side that is
# merely behind is brought up rather than reported; doctor checks it here.
#JULIA_PROJECT=juliaenv
# A SECOND environment is allowed when one dependency exists on only one
# platform (MKL has no aarch64 build, so a shared file fails to instantiate on a
# Mac). It must STRICTLY CONTAIN the base — every package at the same version,
# plus its own extras — which is checked file-to-file in git, on every doctor run
# and on every push. Space-separated, repo-relative.
#JULIA_PROJECT_VARIANTS="juliaenv_gpu"
